---
/**
 * Homepage hero: title, lede, install command, comparison, uck wall.
 *
 * This lives in code rather than in content/index.md because it is markup, not prose:
 * a gradient title, a copy button wired by `data-copy`, and a comparison pane whose
 * tool definitions and API responses are too long for a YAML block scalar. As a
 * component there is no channel by which a content file could inject markup into the
 * page — which is the property the rest of the pipeline relies on.
 *
 * Rendered by Page.astro only when the frontmatter declares `hero: true`, so it cannot
 * appear on a page by accident.
 *
 * The <header class="hero"> wrapper is not decorative: style.css writes its rules as
 * .hero-title, .hero-lede and so on, which match by class, but the layout's own
 * `.hero`-related spacing and the has-hero body class hang on this element being
 * present and named. It used to be emitted by Page.astro around injected markup; here
 * the component owns it so it cannot be forgotten by a caller.
 *
 * Class names here are load-bearing: style.css owns .hero-title, .hero-lede,
 * .install, .uck-wall and friends, and app.js finds the copy button via
 * [data-copy] and the target via the id on the code element.
 */
import Compare from "./Compare.astro";
---
<header class="hero">
<p class="hero-lede"><code>f</code> puts a machine-readable CLI on your <code>PATH</code>. Commands are discovered by running <code>f</code>, not declared in a tool schema.</p>
<div class="install">
  <pre><code id="install-cmd">npm i -g @fcli.dev/f</code></pre>
  <button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="#install-cmd" aria-label="Copy install command">Copy</button>
</div>
<Compare />
</header>
